D=p/'dist'; D.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
def ref(n): return f'<a class="cite" href="#ref{n}" aria-label="Reference {n}">[{n}]</a>'
stages=[
('Acquire','Data acquisition & licensing','A useful model starts with a defensible collection of data. Define domains, languages, permissions, and provenance before ingestion.','Candidate documents and licenses','Versioned, traceable corpus','Source selection; rights review; ingestion','Data stewards, legal review, storage','Licensing, collection, and storage fees','Coverage influences what the model can learn; provenance supports responsible use.',1),
('Prepare','Clean, filter & tokenize','Remove duplicates, filter low-quality material, and isolate evaluation data. A tokenizer converts text into reusable token IDs.','Raw corpus','Filtered token sequences','Deduplication; quality and privacy filters; tokenizer training','CPU workers, storage I/O, data engineers','Processing, repeated scans, and curation labor','Better signal reduces wasted computation; filtering can also remove valuable minority-language data.',1),
('Design','Architecture & initialization','Choose layers, attention, width, vocabulary, and context handling. Initialize trainable parameters and test the configuration at smaller scale.','Token vocabulary and compute budget','Initialized transformer and training recipe','Architecture experiments; optimizer and precision choices','Research time, accelerator memory, pilot runs','Experiment budget and memory footprint','Architecture sets capacity and efficiency; parameter count alone does not establish quality.',6),
('Pretrain','Large-scale pretraining','Predict the next token, measure prediction error, backpropagate gradients, and update weights across repeated batches.','Token batches and initial weights','Base model checkpoints','Forward pass → loss → backward pass → update','GPU/TPU fleet, interconnects, power, checkpoints','Accelerator-hours, downtime, and electricity','Learns broad language patterns, but can reproduce bias and generate unsupported statements.',2),
('Align','Supervised tuning & alignment','Demonstrations teach response behavior. Preference-based methods refine which answers the model favors; recipes differ across developers.','Base model, demonstrations, preferences','Instruction-following model','SFT; preference learning such as RLHF or DPO','Annotators, researchers, reward models, accelerators','Human review, synthetic data generation, and tuning runs','Improves usefulness and behavior; it does not guarantee truth or safety.',8),
('Evaluate','Evaluation, safety & optimization','Measure capability and risk on held-out tasks. Test adversarial cases and deployment settings; recheck after compression or quantization.','Candidate model and test suites','Evidence for release or revision','Benchmarking; red teaming; latency and memory tests','Domain experts, evaluation compute, testing infrastructure','Expert labor, inference runs, and remediation','Separates benchmark success from reliability in real use; failures send work back to earlier stages.',3),
('Deploy','Deployment & monitoring','Serve prompts through the trained model, observe quality and abuse, and manage versions. Feedback informs later development cycles.','Approved weights and serving stack','Generated responses and operational evidence','Batching; caching; access controls; monitoring','Serving GPUs, network, support, storage','Recurring inference, maintenance, and incident response','Latency, reliability, and safeguards affect user experience. Ordinary inference does not update weights.',4)
]
resources=[
('01','Data','Tokens · diversity · provenance','Acquisition, preparation, pretraining, alignment','Licensing, filtering, deduplication, storage, and annotation turn raw material into learning signal. More tokens help only when their mix and quality support the task.','Diverse examples broaden coverage; contamination can inflate evaluation scores.'),
('02','Compute','Accelerators · memory · FLOPs','Architecture, pretraining, alignment, evaluation, inference','GPU/TPU count describes capacity; GPU-hours describe device time; FLOPs describe arithmetic work. Parallelism distributes data or model state. Interconnect bandwidth and checkpoint I/O can limit utilization.','Faster chips help only when data and communication keep them busy.'),
('03','Energy','kWh · cooling · PUE · CO₂e','Every stage using computing infrastructure','Power multiplied by time gives energy. PUE is total facility energy divided by IT energy; it captures overhead such as cooling. Emissions additionally depend on electricity supply and accounting boundaries.','Efficient hardware, workload placement, and reduced rework can lower environmental cost.'),
('04','Time','Elapsed days · device-hours','Pilots through release; ongoing operation','A larger fleet can shorten a run, but synchronization and failures prevent ideal scaling. Experiments, restarts, evaluation, and post-training extend the development calendar.','Time spent validating and recovering protects useful progress.'),
('05','People & finance','Expertise · capital · operating expense','Across the complete lifecycle','Researchers, data engineers, annotators, safety specialists, and operators all contribute. Hardware purchase and cloud rental are alternative accounting approaches; infrastructure needs maintenance.','Skilled review and operations improve reliability beyond what more compute alone can achieve.')
]
refs=[
('Meta. (2024, July 23).','Llama 3.1 model card.','https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.1-405B-Instruct','Official per-model GPU-hours, pretraining tokens, and emissions; see Hardware and Software.'),
('OpenAI. (2023).','GPT-4 technical report.','https://cdn.openai.com/papers/gpt-4.pdf','Scope and limitations; next-token pretraining and RLHF. Proprietary resource details withheld.'),
('Gemini Team. (2023; updated 2024).','Gemini: A family of highly capable multimodal models.','https://storage.googleapis.com/deepmind-media/gemini/gemini_1_report.pdf','Section 3: Ultra uses TPUv4 across data centers. No exact fleet size or run budget given.'),
('Anthropic. (2024, March).','The Claude 3 model family: Opus, Sonnet, Haiku.','https://www-cdn.anthropic.com/de8ba9b01c9ab7cbabf5c33b80b7bbc618857627/Model-Card-Claude-3.pdf','Training approach, Constitutional AI, evaluation, and limitations.'),
('Stanford Institute for Human-Centered Artificial Intelligence. (2025).','The 2025 AI Index report: Chapter 1.','https://hai.stanford.edu/assets/files/hai_ai-index-report-2025_chapter1_final.pdf','Figure 1.3.24, printed p. 43: Epoch AI estimates using cloud compute rental prices; inflation-adjusted.'),
('Vaswani, A., Shazeer, N., Parmar, N., Uszkoreit, J., Jones, L., Gomez, A. N., Kaiser, Ł., & Polosukhin, I. (2017).','Attention is all you need.','https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762','Foundational attention-based transformer architecture.'),
('Hoffmann, J., et al. (2022).','Training compute-optimal large language models.','https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.15556','Compute-budget trade-offs between parameter count and training tokens.'),
('Ouyang, L., et al. (2022).','Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback.','https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02155','Demonstrations, preference rankings, and RLHF; alignment differs from scaling.'),
('Bai, Y., et al. (2022).','Constitutional AI: Harmlessness from AI feedback.','https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.08073','Principle-guided critique, revision, and AI feedback.'),
('Dubey, A., et al. (2024).','The Llama 3 herd of models.','https://arxiv.org/html/2407.21783v3','Sections 3.3 and 4: GPU topology, parallelism, reliability, SFT, and DPO.'),
('Anthropic. (2025, October 23).','Expanding our use of Google Cloud TPUs.','https://www.anthropic.com/news/expanding-our-use-of-google-cloud-tpus-and-services','Company-wide hardware context; not a single-model cost disclosure.')
]
# A precise vector illustration, rather than a decorative AI image.
svg='''<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 600 450" role="img" aria-labelledby="diagram-title diagram-desc"><title id="diagram-title">From tokens to trained weights</title><desc id="diagram-desc">Token inputs flow into transformer layers. Prediction error feeds a weight-update loop. At inference, trained weights generate the next token.</desc><defs><pattern id="grid" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"><path d="M 24 0 L 0 0 0 24" fill="none" stroke="#799ab3" stroke-opacity=".12"/></pattern><marker id="arrow" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="7" refY="4" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0L8 4L0 8" fill="none" stroke="#6ed5cf"/></marker></defs><rect width="600" height="450" fill="url(#grid)"/><g font-family="monospace" font-size="13" fill="#acbdcf"><text x="30" y="42">01 / TOKEN STREAM</text><text x="300" y="42">02 / LEARNED REPRESENTATIONS</text></g><g stroke="#50718d" fill="#112b43"><rect x="28" y="80" width="84" height="43"/><rect x="28" y="145" width="84" height="43"/><rect x="28" y="210" width="84" height="43"/></g><g fill="#e7f1f6" font-family="monospace" font-size="16"><text x="44" y="107">The</text><text x="44" y="172">model</text><text x="44" y="237">learns</text></g><g fill="none" stroke="#6ed5cf" stroke-width="1.5"><path d="M112 101H155V165H210" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/><path d="M112 166H210"/><path d="M112 231H155V166"/></g><g stroke="#6e94b0" fill="#11283f"><path d="M210 105L370 65L465 135L305 175Z"/><path d="M210 145L370 105L465 175L305 215Z"/><path d="M210 185L370 145L465 215L305 255Z"/><path d="M210 225L370 185L465 255L305 295Z"/></g><g fill="none" stroke="#6ed5cf" opacity=".7"><path d="M245 95L340 165M280 87L375 157M315 79L410 149M350 71L445 141M235 124L395 84M260 145L420 105M285 164L445 124"/></g><circle cx="337" cy="182" r="33" fill="#92e4db"/><text x="337" y="188" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace" font-size="16" fill="#092238">Δw</text><g fill="none" stroke="#6ed5cf" stroke-width="1.5"><path d="M465 255H520V335H395" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/><path d="M260 335H175V275H211" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/></g><rect x="260" y="312" width="135" height="46" fill="#142e45" stroke="#6ed5cf"/><text x="327" y="341" fill="#edf5fa" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace" font-size="14">Prediction loss</text><g fill="#acbdcf" font-family="monospace" font-size="12"><text x="28" y="400">TRAINING: UPDATE WEIGHTS ↺</text><text x="28" y="425">INFERENCE: USE LEARNED WEIGHTS → NEXT TOKEN</text></g></svg>'''
(D/'training.svg').write_text(svg)
H='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><meta name="description" content="Explore the training lifecycle, resource economy, and documented costs of large language models. An interactive AIML-501 portfolio artifact by Romires Campelo."><meta name="theme-color" content="#0b2239"><title>The Anatomy of an LLM | Romires Campelo</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css"><script defer src="app.js"></script></head><body><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header><a class="brand" href="#top"><span class="brand-icon">RC</span> RESEARCH / EXPLAINED</a><nav aria-label="Sections"><a href="#pipeline">Pipeline</a><a href="#resources">Resources</a><a href="#evidence">Evidence</a><a href="#lab">Cost lab</a><a href="#references">Sources</a></nav><a class="portfolio" href="https://sites.google.com/view/romirescampelo/artifacts" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Portfolio ↗</a></header><main id="main"><section id="top" class="hero"><div class="hero-inner"><div><p class="eyebrow">AIML–501 / MODEL DEVELOPMENT / IWU</p><h1>The anatomy<br>of an <em>LLM.</em></h1><p class="hero-sub">From raw data to intelligence.</p><p class="hero-copy">The training pipeline, resources, and real costs of generative AI—made visible.</p><a class="button primary" href="#pipeline">Explore the training journey <span>↓</span></a><p class="byline">Romires Campelo <span>•</span> Interactive academic presentation</p></div><figure class="hero-diagram"><img src="training.svg" width="600" height="450" alt="Tokens flow into transformer layers; prediction loss feeds a weight-update loop."><figcaption>THE LEARNING LOOP <span>Conceptual schematic</span></figcaption></figure></div><div class="hero-bottom"><span>01 — DATA TO DEPLOYMENT</span><span>02 — RESOURCES & TRADE-OFFS</span><span>03 — EVIDENCE BEFORE ESTIMATES</span></div></section><section class="intro wrap" aria-labelledby="intro-title"><p class="eyebrow">01 / THE BIG PICTURE</p><div class="section-heading"><h2 id="intro-title">A model is trained.<br>A system is engineered.</h2><p>A generative large language model produces text by learning statistical relationships among tokens. Transformer attention connects information across a context; repeated prediction and optimization adjust its weights. Diverse data supplies examples, while computation makes learning from them possible. <a class="cite" href="#ref6">[6]</a><a class="cite" href="#ref2">[2]</a></p></div><div class="two-up distinction"><article><span class="small-label">TRAINING</span><h3>Change the weights.</h3><p>Examples → prediction error → parameter updates.<br>Development consumes data, experiments, time, energy, and human expertise.</p></article><article><span class="small-label">INFERENCE</span><h3>Use the weights.</h3><p>Prompt → model computation → generated response.<br>Serving creates recurring resource costs; a conversation does not ordinarily retrain the model.</p></article></div></section><section id="pipeline" class="wrap section"><p class="eyebrow">02 / THE TRAINING PIPELINE</p><div class="section-heading"><h2>Seven stages.<br>One connected system.</h2><p>Select a stage to trace its inputs, outputs, and cost drivers. This is a teaching framework: teams iterate, overlap activities, and use different post-training recipes.</p></div><div class="pipeline-controls"><p>READ LEFT TO RIGHT, THEN CONTINUE ↓</p><button id="show-all" class="text-button" aria-pressed="false">Show all stage details</button></div><ol class="pipeline-nav">'''
for i,s in enumerate(stages):
 H+=f'<li><button data-stage="{i}" aria-controls="stage-{i}" aria-pressed="{str(i==0).lower()}"><span>0{i+1}</span>{s[0]}<b aria-hidden="true">→</b></button></li>'
H+='</ol><div class="stage-panels">'
for i,s in enumerate(stages):
 H+=f'<article class="stage" id="stage-{i}"><div class="stage-lead"><span class="stage-number">0{i+1}</span><p class="small-label">{s[0].upper()}</p><h3>{s[1]}</h3><p>{s[2]} {ref(s[9])}</p></div><dl>'
 for label,val in zip(['Input','Output','Activities','Resources','Cost drivers','Quality effect'],s[3:9]): H+=f'<div><dt>{label}</dt><dd>{val}</dd></div>'
 H+='</dl></article>'
H+='''</div><p class="feedback">↶ Evaluation and operational feedback can trigger new data work, tuning, or retraining. Safety review spans the lifecycle.</p><details><summary>Inside a training step: what actually changes?</summary><div class="two-up"><p><strong>Forward pass → loss.</strong> Given prior tokens, the model assigns probabilities to the next token. The training objective penalizes incorrect predictions. A token is a text unit, not necessarily a complete word.</p><p><strong>Backward pass → update.</strong> Backpropagation calculates gradients; an optimizer adjusts parameters. During distributed training, workers must exchange model state or gradients. Saved checkpoints make recovery possible. <a href="#ref2">[2]</a> <a href="#ref10">[10]</a></p></div></details></section><section id="resources" class="resource-section"><div class="wrap section"><p class="eyebrow">03 / THE RESOURCE ECONOMY</p><div class="section-heading"><h2>Beyond the GPU.</h2><p>Resources are inputs to the process. Money and environmental impact are consequences of consuming them. They overlap across stages; these are not slices of a measured budget.</p></div><div class="resource-list">'''
for n,name,units,where,body,effect in resources:
 H+=f'<article class="resource-row"><span class="resource-n">{n}</span><div><h3>{name}</h3><p class="units">{units}</p></div><div><p>{body}</p><p class="impact">{effect}</p></div><p class="stage-map"><span class="small-label">WHERE IT IS USED</span>{where}</p></article>'
H+='''</div><p class="source-note">Resource framework synthesized from model reports and systems research. <a href="#ref1">[1]</a> <a href="#ref3">[3]</a> <a href="#ref10">[10]</a></p><aside class="network-note"><span class="small-label">THE NETWORK ENGINEERING CONNECTION</span><h3>Idle accelerators still cost money.</h3><p>In synchronous training, a slow transfer or failed worker can delay the group. Topology-aware parallelism, reliable interconnects, and fast checkpoint recovery influence useful work per paid hour. Llama 3’s report describes RoCE networking for 405B and up to 16K H100 GPUs—not every chip in the larger cluster. <a href="#ref10">[10]</a></p></aside></div></section><section id="evidence" class="wrap section"><p class="eyebrow">04 / FOUR MODEL CASE STUDIES</p><div class="section-heading"><h2>What do we<br>actually know?</h2><p>Disclosure is uneven. Compare named releases, distinguish evidence from inference, and resist filling gaps with plausible-looking numbers.</p></div><div class="legend"><span><i class="dot official"></i> Officially reported</span><span><i class="dot estimate"></i> Independently estimated</span><span><i class="dot derived"></i> Derived estimate</span><span><i class="dot unknown"></i> Not publicly disclosed</span></div><div class="model-tabs" role="group" aria-label="Select model case study"><button data-model="llama" aria-pressed="true">Meta / Llama 3.1</button><button data-model="gpt" aria-pressed="false">OpenAI / GPT-4</button><button data-model="gemini" aria-pressed="false">Google / Gemini Ultra</button><button data-model="claude" aria-pressed="false">Anthropic / Claude 3</button></div><div id="model-detail" aria-live="polite"></div><noscript><p>Enable JavaScript for the model selector. The comparison and references below remain available.</p></noscript><div class="chart-heading"><h3>Training compute, priced consistently.</h3><label for="metric">Compare by <select id="metric"><option value="cost">Estimated compute rental cost</option><option value="hours">Official GPU-hours</option><option value="tokens">Official pretraining tokens</option><option value="emissions">Official location-based emissions</option></select></label></div><figure class="comparison"><div id="chart" aria-live="polite"></div><figcaption id="chart-note"></figcaption></figure><div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Evidence ledger — named model releases</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Model</th><th scope="col">Release</th><th scope="col">Compute rental estimate¹</th><th scope="col">Reported training GPU-hours</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Llama 3.1 405B</th><td>2024</td><td>≈ $170M [5]</td><td>30.84M H100-hours [1]</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">GPT-4</th><td>2023</td><td>≈ $79M [5]</td><td>Not publicly disclosed [2]</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Gemini 1.0 Ultra</th><td>2024²</td><td>≈ $192M [5]</td><td>Not publicly disclosed [3]</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Claude 3 Opus</th><td>2024</td><td>Not publicly disclosed [4]</td><td>Not publicly disclosed [4]</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="source-note">¹ Epoch AI estimates reproduced in the 2025 AI Index, Fig. 1.3.24 (inflation-adjusted USD; cloud compute rental basis). These are not audited bills or total R&D budgets. <a href="#ref5">[5]</a> ² Gemini was announced in December 2023; the Index dates Ultra to its 2024 release.</p></section><section id="lab" class="lab"><div class="wrap section"><p class="eyebrow">05 / INTERACTIVE RESOURCE LAB</p><div class="section-heading"><h2>Make the costs<br>tangible.</h2><p>An illustrative fleet, not a reconstruction of a proprietary training run. Change the assumptions and see how device time, rental expense, and electricity respond.</p></div><div class="lab-layout"><form id="estimator"><div class="inputs">'''
for ident,label,value,mi,ma,step,unit in [('gpus','GPU count',1024,1,100000,1,'devices'),('days','Run duration',30,1,730,1,'days'),('rate','GPU rental rate',3,0,100,.1,'USD / GPU-hour'),('power','Average GPU power',700,1,2000,1,'watts / GPU'),('pue','Facility PUE',1.2,1,3,.01,'facility / IT energy'),('electric','Electricity rate',.12,0,2,.01,'USD / kWh')]:
 H+=f'<label for="{ident}">{label}<span class="input-line"><input id="{ident}" type="number" min="{mi}" max="{ma}" step="{step}" value="{value}" required><span>{unit}</span></span></label>'
H+='''</div><button type="reset" class="text-button">Reset assumptions ↺</button></form><div class="lab-output" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"><p class="small-label">ILLUSTRATIVE RESULTS</p><p id="lab-error" role="status"></p><div><span>Compute rental expense</span><strong id="rental">$2,211,840</strong></div><div class="output-pair"><p><span>Accelerator time</span><strong id="hours">737,280</strong><small>GPU-hours</small></p><p><span>GPU-based facility estimate</span><strong id="energy">619,315</strong><small>kWh</small></p></div><p class="electricity">Electricity equivalent: <b id="bill">$74,318</b><br><small>Shown separately. Do not add it to rental if already included.</small></p></div></div><details class="formulas"><summary>Formulas, units, and boundaries</summary><p><code>H = GPUs × days × 24</code><br><code>Rental (USD) = H × USD per GPU-hour</code><br><code>Energy (kWh) ≈ H × watts / 1,000 × PUE</code><br><code>Electricity equivalent (USD) = kWh × USD per kWh</code></p><p>Assumes a constant allocated fleet and continuous operation. Enter average draw, not peak TDP, for an energy scenario. Applying PUE to GPU-only energy omits CPU, storage, and network IT loads; this is a simplified estimate, not a facility meter reading. Rental rates are hypothetical, not current quotes. Labor, licensing, pilot runs, post-training, hardware manufacturing, and ongoing inference are excluded. To estimate operational carbon, multiply kWh by a stated emissions factor; no factor is assumed here.</p></details></div></section><section id="tradeoffs" class="wrap section"><p class="eyebrow">06 / THE ENGINEERING TRADE-OFFS</p><div class="section-heading"><h2>More is a choice.<br>Better is a test.</h2><p>More resources can improve capability, but do not automatically produce a better model. Scaling laws describe empirical trends under assumptions; they are not guarantees of correctness or safety. <a href="#ref7">[7]</a><a href="#ref8">[8]</a></p></div><div class="trade-grid"><article><span class="small-label">CAPACITY ↔ DATA</span><h3>Balance the two.</h3><p>Larger models require sufficient training signal. Under a fixed compute budget, allocating more tokens to a smaller model can outperform simply increasing parameters. Data quality and intended use shape the decision. <a href="#ref7">[7]</a></p></article><article><span class="small-label">SCALE ↔ EFFICIENCY</span><h3>Count useful work.</h3><p>More chips can shorten elapsed time, while communication and failures reduce the gain. Improving utilization can yield more learning from the same hardware budget. <a href="#ref10">[10]</a></p></article><article><span class="small-label">CAPABILITY ↔ SAFETY</span><h3>Evaluate both.</h3><p>A strong benchmark score does not demonstrate safe behavior. Preference learning, adversarial testing, and monitoring address different failure modes. Constitutional AI adds principle-guided feedback. <a href="#ref8">[8]</a><a href="#ref9">[9]</a></p></article></div><div class="trade-flow" aria-label="Trade-off relationships"><div><b>Data quality + model capacity</b><span>shape learning opportunities ↓</span></div><div><b>Compute × training time</b><span>consume money and energy ↓</span></div><div><b>Optimization + evaluation</b><span>test whether capability and reliability improved</span></div></div><p class="callout">Diminishing returns: each additional unit of computation may buy a smaller improvement. The right stopping point depends on the task, cost, and measured outcomes.</p></section><section id="reflection" class="reflection"><div class="wrap section"><p class="eyebrow">07 / SYNTHESIS & REFLECTION</p><h2>The model is only<br>part of the system.</h2><div class="two-up"><div><h3>Three ideas to carry forward</h3><ol class="takeaways"><li><b>Data quality is an engineering decision.</b> Curation determines the signal that expensive hardware processes.</li><li><b>Infrastructure changes the economics.</b> Memory, networking, reliability, and efficiency shape useful progress.</li><li><b>Evidence needs boundaries.</b> Training and inference, official disclosures and estimates, and capability and safety answer different questions.</li></ol><p>Frontier model development joins data systems, high-performance computing, optimization, safety engineering, and infrastructure operations.</p></div><article class="reflection-copy"><p class="small-label">LEARNING REFLECTION</p><p>I view LLM training as a systems-engineering problem. From a telecommunications and IT perspective, the connection between network design and useful accelerator time is especially meaningful: moving data reliably is part of making computation productive.</p><p>The central learning challenge is comparing figures that measure different things. I would address it by checking each number’s scope, unit, source, and disclosure status before drawing conclusions. GPU-hours, calendar duration, rental estimates, and carbon accounting cannot substitute for one another.</p><p>For practical engineering decisions, I would prioritize data quality, measure utilization before expanding capacity, and assess reliability alongside capability. This framework connects model development to responsible resource planning, rather than treating a larger model as an automatic improvement.</p></article></div><details><summary>How This Information Was Organized</summary><p>Stages classify <em>when and why work happens</em>. Resource categories classify <em>what that work consumes</em>. Quality effects describe <em>what may change</em>. Case studies are <em>evidence about named releases</em>, not additional stages. Cross-links connect each resource to multiple stages because training is not a set of isolated purchases.</p><p>For example, GPUs are hardware resources, GPU-hours measure time on that hardware, dollars express an accounting estimate, and CO₂e represents environmental impact. None belongs in a single interchangeable “cost” column. The evidence labels distinguish developer reports, external estimates, transparent calculations, and missing disclosure. Evaluation spans development and operations even though it appears as a separate stage for clarity.</p></details></div></section><section id="references" class="wrap section"><p class="eyebrow">08 / REFERENCES & RESEARCH NOTES</p><div class="section-heading"><h2>Follow the evidence.</h2><p>Research reviewed November 16, 2026. Historical releases are intentionally fixed for comparability. Source numbers beside claims link to this list. Bibliographic entries use a consistent author–date style; long author lists are abbreviated.</p></div><ol class="reference-list">'''
for i,(author,title,url,note) in enumerate(refs,1): H+=f'<li id="ref{i}"><span class="ref-number">[{i}]</span><div>{author} <a href="{url}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><cite>{title}</cite> ↗</a><p>{note}</p></div></li>'
H+='''</ol><details><summary>Research limitations & calculation notes</summary><p>“Not publicly disclosed” refers to the cited release documentation, not a claim that no estimate exists anywhere. The chart shows only compatible metrics with traceable sources. No missing value is replaced by zero. Published cost estimates have uncertainty that is not quantified in the source figure.</p><p>For Llama 3.1 405B, dividing 30.84 million GPU-hours by a hypothetical constant fleet of 16,384 GPUs and 24 hours yields about 78.4 equivalent days. This is a derived illustration, not reported calendar training duration. The 54-day reliability observation in the Llama paper is a sample window, not the entire run. Location-based and market-based emissions are separate accounting views; renewable matching does not mean that no electricity was consumed.</p><p>The artifact uses original explanatory text, diagrams, and calculations. It does not claim to reproduce proprietary training recipes, measure model quality, or estimate the full cost of an AI company.</p></details><button class="button print-button" id="print">Print / save as PDF</button></section></main><footer><span>ROMIRES CAMPELO / AIML–501</span><a href="#top">Back to the beginning ↑</a><span>Data → training → evidence</span></footer></body></html>'''
(D/'index.html').write_text(H)
